/* Final "Interview loop" week for the swe, sd and fin programs. First option in every multiple choice is the correct one (shuffled at runtime). */
(function () {
  const { T, Q, F, O, RK, M, BN, BUG, NUM, L } = GR.ivKit;

  /* ================= SOFTWARE ENGINEERING ================= */
  GR.courseAddUnit("swe", {
    title: "Interview loop", sub: "Design, debugging and your own projects, out loud",
    lessons: [
      L("The questions they actually ask", "swe.practices", [
        T("<p>Outside the coding round, a software loop is mostly three conversations: <b>how do you design things</b>, <b>how do you fix things</b>, and <b>what have you actually built</b>. The common thread in strong answers is that they <b>name the tradeoff</b> instead of crowning a winner. Below are questions that are commonly asked, with the answer shape that tends to land.</p>"),
        Q("\"Tell me about a time you disagreed with a teammate on a technical decision.\" Which answer is strongest?", ["Describe both options, how you compared them with a prototype or data, how the call was made, and how you committed to it","Explain why your option was technically superior and walk through where the other person's reasoning broke down, so it is clear you were right","Say you rarely disagree because you defer to the senior engineer's judgment on technical questions and focus on delivering your assigned work","Describe how you escalated to your manager right away so the decision was settled quickly, and note that the disagreement ended once they ruled"], "They are testing how you handle disagreement: evidence over ego, and commitment after the call is made."),
        Q("\"What happens when you type a URL into a browser and press Enter?\" Strongest opening?", ["Walk the path in layers: DNS lookup, TCP and TLS, HTTP request, server work, response, rendering; offer to go deeper on any layer","Start with the server: explain how the web server parses the request and queries the database, then mention the browser briefly at the end","Go straight into how the browser's rendering engine builds the DOM and CSSOM, and treat networking as an implementation detail","Say it depends on the browser and the site, so there is no single answer worth describing in an interview"], "A layered walk shows breadth and lets the interviewer steer you to the depth they want."),
        Q("\"Design a URL shortener.\" What should your first two minutes contain?", ["Clarify functional needs and scale (reads versus writes, lifetime, custom aliases), then sketch the API","Draw the full database schema with every column and index, since the data model is what everything else depends on and must be fixed first","Name a specific cloud vendor and its managed services so the interviewer knows which stack you would deploy on from day one","Pick a hashing algorithm such as MD5 or SHA-256 and defend why it avoids collisions before discussing any requirements"], "Requirements and scale numbers drive every later choice; jumping to components first is the classic mistake."),
        Q("\"Latency doubled right after a deploy.\" What do you do first?", ["Check whether users are hurt now and roll back if so, then use metrics and the diff to localize the cause","Read the whole diff line by line before touching anything, because a rollback would destroy the evidence you need to find the cause","Restart every server in the fleet to see if the latency clears, then investigate only if it comes back after the restart","Wait an hour to see whether the latency settles down by itself before deciding whether the deploy is worth investigating"], "Mitigate first, diagnose second. A rollback is cheap, and the evidence stays in the logs."),
        T("<p><b>Debugging answers</b> follow one loop: reproduce, localize, form one hypothesis, change one thing, verify. Say it in that order and you sound like someone who has been paged at 3 a.m. before (and slept badly after).</p>"),
        Q("\"How would you test a function that sorts a list of integers?\"", ["Empty, one element, duplicates, sorted, reversed, huge input, plus a check that output is ordered and a permutation","Give it a handful of random lists and confirm by eye that each result looks sorted, since sorting is simple enough not to need more","Compare the output against one carefully chosen hard-coded answer and treat a match as proof that the function works for every input","Run it once on the largest list you can generate and check that it finishes quickly, since performance is the main risk in sorting"], "Edge cases plus a property that must always hold beats a few spot checks."),
        Q("\"What is the difference between a process and a thread?\"", ["Processes have separate memory spaces; threads in one process share memory, which is faster to share but needs synchronization","Threads are simply processes that the OS schedules more quickly, and each thread gets its own private copy of the heap and globals","A process can only run on a single CPU core at a time, while a thread can be spread across several cores to run in parallel","Threads are invisible to the operating system and scheduled entirely by the language runtime, so they never need synchronization"], "Shared memory is both the benefit and the hazard of threads."),
        Q("A table has an index on <code>(last_name, first_name)</code>. Which query can use it efficiently?", ["<code>WHERE last_name = 'Lee'</code>", "<code>WHERE first_name = 'Ana'</code>", "<code>WHERE first_name LIKE '%na'</code>", "<code>WHERE UPPER(last_name) = 'LEE'</code>"], "A composite index works from its leftmost column. Skipping it, a leading wildcard, or wrapping the column in a function defeats the index."),
        Q("A client retries <code>POST /payments</code> after a timeout. How do you avoid charging twice?", ["Require an idempotency key and store the result against it server-side", "Tell clients never to retry", "Make the endpoint a GET so it is safe to repeat", "Add a longer timeout on the client"], "Retries are inevitable, so make the operation safe to repeat."),
        Q("\"When would you choose composition over inheritance?\"", ["When you need to combine behaviors flexibly or the 'is-a' relationship is shaky, since composition avoids deep brittle hierarchies","Never, because inheritance gives you code reuse for free and a deep class hierarchy is always easier to read than assembled parts","Only when the language has no class keyword, since composition exists as a workaround for languages that cannot express inheritance","When you want every method to be public, since composition exposes the internals of the wrapped objects to every caller"], "Inheritance couples a subclass to its parent's internals; composition keeps pieces swappable."),
        M("Match each ACID letter to what it guarantees.", [["Atomicity", "All of a transaction's writes happen or none do"], ["Consistency", "A transaction moves data from one valid state to another"], ["Isolation", "Concurrent transactions do not see each other's half-finished work"], ["Durability", "Once committed, the data survives a crash"]], "Four separate promises. Isolation is the one with adjustable levels."),
        BN("Which store is the more natural first fit?", ["Relational database", "Key-value or document store"], [["Bank transfers between accounts", 0], ["Orders joined to customers and products", 0], ["Reporting with ad hoc joins", 0], ["User session tokens with a TTL", 1], ["Product catalog pages fetched by ID", 1], ["Shopping cart blobs per user", 1]], "Relational wins when you need transactions and joins; key-value wins on simple lookups at scale."),
        F("Fill in the clause that filters <b>groups</b>.", "SELECT user_id, COUNT(*)\nFROM orders\n___ user_id\n___ COUNT(*) > 5;", ["GROUP BY", "HAVING"], ["WHERE", "ORDER BY"], "GROUP BY forms the groups; HAVING filters them after aggregation. WHERE filters rows before."),
        BUG("This function has a classic Python bug. Tap the line.", ["def add_tag(tag, tags=[]):", "    tags.append(tag)", "    return tags", "", "print(add_tag('a'))", "print(add_tag('b'))"], 0, "The default list is created once and shared across calls, so the second call prints ['a', 'b']. Default to None and create the list inside."),
        RK("Rank these reads from fastest to slowest (typical).", ["CPU cache hit", "Main memory read", "SSD random read", "Round trip within one data center", "Round trip across an ocean"], "Nanoseconds, then roughly 100 ns, then tens of microseconds, then about half a millisecond, then tens to hundreds of milliseconds."),
        NUM("A service gets 5,000,000 requests a day, spread evenly. About how many requests per second on average?", 58, "5,000,000 / 86,400 is about 57.9. Remember 86,400 seconds in a day.", null, 1, "req/s"),
        Q("\"Tell me about a project you are proud of.\" Which structure works best?", ["Problem and why it mattered, your role, the hardest decision and its tradeoff, a measurable result, and what you would change","A tour of every technology, framework and tool used in the project, in the order you adopted them, so the interviewer sees your breadth","A description of what the team accomplished and how well everyone worked together, keeping the focus on the group instead of yourself","A summary of the product roadmap and the business strategy behind it, with the feature timeline for the next several quarters"], "Interviewers want ownership, judgment and honesty about hindsight."),
        Q("A reviewer rejects your approach and you disagree. Best response?", ["Ask what concern is driving it, share your evidence, find a version both satisfy, and defer if it is a style preference","Merge it anyway once someone else on the team approves it, since two approvals outweigh one reviewer's objection","Rewrite it their way without comment and keep quiet about it, which avoids conflict even if you still think your approach is better","Keep arguing in the review thread until they concede, since a correct approach should not need to change to suit a preference"], "Code review is collaboration; separate real risks from preferences."),
        Q("Which status code fits a successful <code>POST</code> that creates a resource?", ["201 Created", "200 OK with an empty body", "204 No Content", "302 Found"], "201 signals creation and usually carries a Location header or the new resource."),
        Q("A page runs 1 query for 100 orders, then 100 more queries for each order's customer. What is this called and the usual fix?", ["The N+1 query problem; fetch with a join or batch the IDs", "Cache stampede; add a lock", "Deadlock; reorder the queries", "Thrashing; add memory"], "Many tiny queries cost round trips. Join once or batch with an IN list."),
        O("Order a calm response to a production incident.", ["Acknowledge and assess user impact", "Mitigate (roll back, flip a flag, shed load)", "Communicate status to stakeholders", "Find the root cause with evidence", "Fix permanently and add a regression test", "Write a blameless postmortem"], "Stop the bleeding before finding the cause; learn after."),
        Q("\"How do you decide when a service should be split off from a monolith?\"", ["When independent scaling, team ownership or release cadence pain justifies the added operational cost","Whenever a single file or module grows past a few thousand lines, since size alone shows that the code is ready to be separated","Always, since microservices are the modern standard and every new system should start as many small independently deployed services","Never, since a monolith avoids network hops and is therefore always faster, simpler and cheaper to run at any scale"], "Splitting buys independence and costs network calls, deployment complexity and harder debugging."),
        T("<p>One last pattern. When asked <b>\"which is better, A or B?\"</b>, answer with a template: <i>\"I'd pick A because of X, at the cost of Y, unless Z changes.\"</i> It sounds senior because it is.</p>"),
      ], "learn"),

      L("Say it out loud", "swe.sysdesign", [
        T("<p>Interviewers can't see your thoughts, only your structure. Rehearse the <b>skeleton</b> until it is automatic, then spend your brainpower on the content. Silence while thinking is fine; say \"give me a moment to think\" and it reads as deliberate.</p>"),
        O("Order a system design answer.", ["Clarify requirements and scale", "Estimate load and storage", "Sketch the API and data model", "Draw the high-level components", "Deep dive into the biggest bottleneck", "Discuss failure modes and tradeoffs"], "Requirements first, details last. The deep dive is where you show depth."),
        O("Order an API design walk-through.", ["Identify the resources and the callers", "Define endpoints and verbs", "Specify request, response and error shapes", "Add pagination, auth and rate limits", "Plan versioning and idempotency"], "Start from the consumer's needs, then harden the contract."),
        O("Order a debugging walk-through.", ["Restate the symptom and its impact", "Reproduce it or find the first bad time", "Form a hypothesis from logs, metrics and recent changes", "Test one hypothesis at a time", "Fix, add a regression test, record the cause"], "Evidence, then hypothesis, then one change at a time."),
        RK("Rank by how much they usually improve read latency (largest effect first).", ["Serve from an in-memory cache", "Add the right database index", "Add a read replica", "Compress response payloads"], "A cache hit skips the database. An index turns a scan into a lookup. A replica spreads load. Compression helps transfer time."),
        RK("Rank these rollout strategies from safest to riskiest.", ["Feature flag at 1% of users", "Canary on one region", "Rolling update across all servers", "Big-bang release to everyone"], "The smaller the initial blast radius, the safer."),
        M("Match each design prompt to the key point a strong answer must hit.", [["Design a rate limiter", "Algorithm choice (such as token bucket), the limiting key, and where shared state lives"], ["Design a URL shortener", "Unique ID generation and a read-heavy caching strategy"], ["Design a chat app", "Persistent connections, message ordering and offline delivery"], ["Design a notification system", "Queue-based fan-out with retries and deduplication"]], "Each classic prompt has one or two crux decisions. Name them early."),
        M("Match each behavioral prompt to what a strong answer centers on.", [["Tell me about a conflict", "Your actions and what you would repeat, not who was wrong"], ["Tell me about a failure", "Ownership plus a habit you changed"], ["Why this company?", "A specific link between their problem and your experience"], ["Walk me through a project", "Your decisions and tradeoffs, with numbers"]], "Every behavioral answer is really a test of judgment and self-awareness."),
        BN("Say it, or skip it, in a spoken design answer?", ["Say it", "Skip it"], [["\"Assuming 10x read-to-write ratio, so I'll cache.\"", 0], ["\"The tradeoff is staleness for speed.\"", 0], ["\"If this component fails, we degrade to X.\"", 0], ["A 90-second tangent on one library's internals", 1], ["Naming ten technologies with no reasons", 1], ["\"Obviously it should use microservices.\"", 1]], "State assumptions, tradeoffs and failure behavior. Skip trivia and unexplained buzzwords."),
        Q("The interviewer says \"design a notification system\" and goes quiet. Your best first sentence?", ["\"Let me clarify: which channels, what volume, and do we need delivery guarantees?\"","\"I'd start with Kafka as the backbone, and then we can work out the channels and the volume from there.\"","\"Here is the database schema I would use, starting with a notifications table and a table of user preferences.\"","\"Could you give me a more specific question first, since this one is too open-ended to answer well?\""], "Questions about scope and guarantees set up everything that follows."),
        Q("How should you handle a requirement you are unsure about?", ["State your assumption out loud and proceed, and tell them you will revisit if it is wrong","Stop and wait for the interviewer to tell you the answer, since any assumption could send the whole design in the wrong direction","Quietly pick whichever option seems most likely and keep going without mentioning it, so the flow of your answer is not interrupted","Skip that part of the design entirely and focus on the parts you are sure about, returning to it only if asked"], "Visible assumptions keep you moving and keep the interviewer informed."),
        NUM("A system gets 86,400,000 requests per day, evenly spread. Average requests per second?", 1000, "86,400,000 / 86,400 = 1,000. That is a handy anchor: 1M a day is about 12 per second.", null, 0, "req/s"),
        NUM("Peak traffic is about 3 times average. With 1,000 average requests per second, plan for how many at peak?", 3000, "1,000 x 3 = 3,000. Design for the peak, not the mean.", null, 0, "req/s"),
        NUM("100 million records at 1 KB each need roughly how many GB of storage (before replication)?", 100, "100,000,000 x 1 KB = 100,000,000 KB = 100 GB, in decimal units.", null, 0, "GB"),
        F("Fill in the status code and the retry rule.", "A client that sends too many requests gets HTTP ___ Too Many Requests.\nRetries use ___ backoff plus random jitter.", ["429", "exponential"], ["503", "linear", "404", "constant"], "429 is the standard rate-limit response. Exponential backoff with jitter prevents synchronized retry storms."),
        BUG("This withdraw function has a race condition. Tap the line that makes it unsafe.", ["def withdraw(acct, amt):", "    bal = db.get_balance(acct)", "    if bal >= amt:", "        db.set_balance(acct, bal - amt)", "        return True", "    return False"], 1, "Reading the balance outside a lock or transaction means two requests can both see enough money. Use an atomic conditional update or a transaction with row locking."),
        Q("You propose a cache. The interviewer asks \"what could go wrong?\" Strongest answer?", ["Stale data, stampedes when keys expire together, and write inconsistency; mitigate with TTL jitter and write-through or invalidation","Nothing significant, because a cache only sits in front of the database and can only ever make reads faster, never make them wrong","Memory might run out when the cache fills, so you would simply provision a larger cache node and the problem would be solved","The cache might be attacked from outside, so you would place it behind a firewall and encrypt its contents; staleness is not a real concern"], "Caching trades freshness for speed; naming the failure modes is the point."),
        Q("Which sentence best states a tradeoff?", ["\"I'd use a queue so writes survive spikes, at the cost of eventual consistency.\"","\"A queue is clearly the best choice here, and I would put one between the API and the workers.\"","\"Queues are what most large companies use for this kind of workload, so I would follow that convention.\"","\"We can decide on the queue later, once we see how traffic behaves, and keep the writes synchronous for now.\""], "Choice, reason and cost, in one breath."),
        Q("The interviewer pushes back on your design. What is the best reaction?", ["Restate their concern, test it against your design, and adapt if it holds","Defend the original design harder, since changing it under pressure suggests you did not think it through the first time","Abandon the design immediately and start over with whatever approach they seem to prefer, to show you are flexible","Ask them what they would do and copy it, since the interviewer likely already has the answer in mind"], "Pushback is usually a hint or a probe of flexibility. Reason about it openly."),
        Q("How should you close a system design answer?", ["Summarize the design, name the top risk and what you would build or measure next","Say \"that's it\" and wait for questions, so the interviewer can take the conversation wherever they like","Apologize for the parts you did not get to and list everything you would have covered with more time","Ask how you did and whether there is anything you should have said, so you can adjust in later rounds"], "A crisp recap with risks shows ownership of the design."),
        O("Order a behavioral (STAR) answer.", ["Situation: brief context", "Task: what you owned", "Action: what you did and why", "Result: the outcome, with a number", "Reflection: what you would change"], "Spend most of your time on Action."),
        Q("Which is the strongest measurable result for a project story?", ["\"Cut p95 latency from 800 ms to 220 ms for 4M weekly users.\"","\"It became much faster and the whole team agreed it was a big improvement for users.\"","\"People seemed happy with it, and several teammates told me it was the best release of the year.\"","\"We shipped on time and under budget, and the stakeholders were satisfied with the end result.\""], "Numbers make claims checkable. Include the before, the after and the scale."),
        T("<p>Practice tip: record yourself walking through one design in 15 minutes, then listen for filler and missing tradeoffs. It is humbling and extremely effective.</p>"),
      ], "learn"),

      L("Follow-ups they throw at you", "swe.db", [
        T("<p>The first answer gets you on the board. The <b>follow-up</b> shows whether you understand it. These are commonly asked probes, and they get harder as you answer well, which is the interviewer's way of saying nice job.</p>"),
        NUM("Your cache hits 95% of the time and the database sees 1,000 misses per second. How many total requests per second arrive?", 20000, "Misses are 5% of traffic, so total = 1,000 / 0.05 = 20,000.", null, 0, "req/s"),
        Q("A user updates their profile and immediately sees the old one, because reads come from a lagging replica. Best targeted fix?", ["Read-your-writes: send that user's reads to the primary for a short window after a write", "Remove replicas entirely", "Increase the cache TTL", "Tell users to refresh"], "Read-your-writes consistency fixes the user-visible case without losing replica scaling."),
        Q("Two requests update the same row and one update is silently lost. Which fix targets this?", ["Optimistic locking with a version column, retrying on conflict", "A larger database instance", "More indexes", "Longer timeouts"], "Check the version at write time; if it changed, someone else won, so retry."),
        Q("\"Why not index every column?\"", ["Each index slows writes, uses storage and widens the planner's choices; index for real query patterns","Indexes are free to maintain but hard to create, so you should add one to every column only after the table is fully loaded","Databases allow only one index per table, so adding more than one would fail and force you to choose the single best column","Indexes break transactions, because every index forces a separate lock that cannot be rolled back with the row it points to"], "Indexes speed reads and tax writes. Add them for measured queries."),
        Q("Offset pagination (<code>OFFSET 1000000</code>) gets slower on deep pages. Fix?", ["Keyset (cursor) pagination: query rows after the last seen key", "Raise the page size", "Add a larger cache", "Sort in the application"], "Offset makes the database skip rows it still has to read. Keyset seeks directly."),
        Q("After an outage every client retries at once and the service dies again. Which set of measures helps most?", ["Exponential backoff with jitter, circuit breakers, and load shedding", "Retry immediately and more often", "Remove timeouts", "Add more logging"], "Spread out and slow down retries, and fail fast to protect the service."),
        Q("\"How would you shard a users table?\" Best shard key properties?", ["High cardinality, even spread, and matching the main access pattern; watch hot keys and cross-shard queries","Country, since it is easy to explain and gives each region its own shard, which keeps most traffic local to that data center","Signup date, so new users land together on the newest shard and older shards stay quiet, which keeps every shard evenly loaded","Whatever the first column of the table is, since the shard key only affects where rows are stored and not how queries perform"], "A bad key creates hot shards; a good one keeps most queries on one shard."),
        Q("\"Can you guarantee exactly-once delivery?\" Strongest answer?", ["Effectively yes with at-least-once delivery plus idempotent consumers", "Yes, any good broker does it", "No, so messages will always be lost", "Only if you use a database"], "True exactly-once across a network is a myth; deduplication makes the effect exactly-once."),
        Q("A queue consumer crashes halfway through a message. What should have happened?", ["The message was not acknowledged until processing finished, so it is redelivered and handled idempotently","The message is lost, which is acceptable, since the producer will eventually notice the missing result and send a fresh copy","The broker keeps retrying forever on the same crashed consumer, so no other consumer ever sees the message until a restart","The producer resends it automatically as soon as the consumer disappears, because producers always track whether each message was processed"], "Ack after the work is done, and make the work safe to repeat."),
        Q("Within one transaction, a row is read twice and returns different values. Which anomaly is this?", ["Non-repeatable read", "Dirty read", "Phantom read", "Lost update"], "Another transaction committed a change in between. REPEATABLE READ or stricter prevents it."),
        M("Match each concurrency anomaly to its description.", [["Dirty read", "Reading another transaction's uncommitted change"], ["Non-repeatable read", "Same row, different value on a second read"], ["Phantom read", "A repeated range query returns new rows"], ["Lost update", "Two writers overwrite each other's change"]], "Each isolation level rules out more of these."),
        RK("Rank isolation levels from weakest to strongest.", ["Read uncommitted", "Read committed", "Repeatable read", "Serializable"], "Each step forbids more anomalies, usually at a cost in concurrency."),
        NUM("A B-tree index has a fanout of about 100 children per node. About how many levels cover a 50-million-row table?", 4, "100 x 100 x 100 = 1 million, which is too few; 100^4 = 100 million is enough. So 4 levels.", null, 0, "levels"),
        NUM("A primary writes 200 MB/s but a replica link carries only 100 MB/s. After 60 seconds, how many MB of backlog?", 6000, "The gap is 100 MB/s, and 100 x 60 = 6,000 MB. Replication lag grows without bound.", null, 0, "MB"),
        NUM("Three services in series each have 99.9% availability. Overall availability, in percent, to one decimal?", 99.7, "0.999 cubed is about 0.997, so 99.7%. Dependencies in series multiply failure risk.", null, 0.05, "%"),
        NUM("Two independent replicas each have 99% availability and either can serve a request. Combined availability in percent?", 99.99, "Both fail with probability 0.01 x 0.01 = 0.0001, so availability is 99.99%.", null, 0.005, "%"),
        BUG("This code is slow on large lists. Tap the line responsible.", ["orders = db.query('SELECT * FROM orders')", "for o in orders:", "    o.user = db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = %s', o.user_id)", "    print(o.user.name, o.total)"], 2, "One query per order is the N+1 pattern. Join, or fetch all users with one IN query."),
        BUG("This endpoint is vulnerable. Tap the line.", ["def get_user(conn, name):", "    cur = conn.cursor()", "    cur.execute(f\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '{name}'\")", "    return cur.fetchone()"], 2, "String-building the query allows SQL injection. Use a parameterized query."),
        O("Order a zero-downtime column rename (expand and contract).", ["Add the new column (nullable)", "Deploy code that writes both columns", "Backfill old rows into the new column", "Switch reads to the new column", "Stop writing the old column", "Drop the old column"], "Each step is safe to deploy and roll back independently."),
        BN("During a network partition, which would you rather favor?", ["Consistency", "Availability"], [["Account balance transfers", 0], ["Seat reservation for a flight", 0], ["Last item of inventory", 0], ["Shopping cart contents", 1], ["Social media like counts", 1], ["Product view counters", 1]], "Money and unique resources need correctness; counters and carts can reconcile later."),
        F("Complete the optimistic-locking update.", "UPDATE items\nSET qty = qty - 1, version = version + 1\nWHERE id = 7 AND ___ = 3;", ["version"], ["qty", "id", "rowid"], "The update only applies if nobody changed the row since we read version 3. Zero rows updated means a conflict."),
        Q("\"Give an example of violating the Liskov Substitution Principle.\"", ["A <code>Square</code> subclassing <code>Rectangle</code> so setting width also changes height, breaking callers","A class with a very long name that describes everything it does, which makes callers depend on the name instead of the behavior","A subclass that adds a new method the parent does not have, so callers holding the parent type cannot reach the extra behavior","An interface that declares only a single method, since small interfaces force every implementer to behave identically in all cases"], "A subtype must be usable anywhere its parent is, without surprising callers."),
        Q("You must remove a field from a public API. What is the responsible path?", ["Deprecate with notice, monitor who still uses it, version the change, then remove", "Delete it and bump the patch version", "Keep it forever with no notice", "Rename it silently"], "Breaking consumers is a trust cost; give migration time and data."),
        Q("A test passes locally and fails randomly in CI. Your first suspicion?", ["Flakiness from shared state, timing, ordering or randomness; isolate and make the test deterministic","CI is broken and unreliable, so skip the test in CI until the infrastructure team has had time to fix the environment","Re-run the job until it passes and merge once it is green, since a test that passes at least once cannot be hiding a bug","Delete the test, since a test that cannot be trusted gives less value than none and code review will catch the same problems"], "A flaky test hides real bugs; fix the cause instead of masking it."),
        Q("\"What does the CAP theorem actually say?\"", ["When a network partition happens, a distributed system must choose between consistency and availability","Pick any two of consistency, availability and partition tolerance at all times, so every system permanently gives up one of the three","Databases can never be consistent once they are distributed across more than one machine, so consistency is always sacrificed first","Partitions never happen in practice on modern cloud networks, so the theorem only matters for systems built on unreliable hardware"], "Partitions are a fact, so the real choice is C or A while one is happening."),
        T("<p>If you do not know a follow-up, say what you <i>do</i> know, reason from first principles, and flag the guess. \"I'm not certain, but I'd expect X because Y\" beats confident nonsense every time.</p>"),
      ], "drill"),

      L("Mock loop", "swe.sysdesign", [
        T("<p>Full-round simulation: treat each card as the next question in the loop. No hints, no do-overs. Aim to answer in the shape of a spoken response.</p>"),
        Q("Round 1: \"Design a rate limiter for an API.\" Which opening clarification is most useful?", ["Is it per user, per IP or per API key, and what happens when the limit is hit?","Which programming language and framework will we build the limiter in, and does the team already have experience with them?","Is the API written as REST, GraphQL or gRPC, and does it run behind a load balancer?","How many engineers will build and maintain it, and how long do we have before the first release?"], "The limiting key and the over-limit behavior define the whole design."),
        Q("A token bucket limits to 100 requests per minute with bursts allowed. Why choose it over a fixed window counter?", ["It allows controlled bursts and avoids the double-burst at window boundaries", "It uses no memory", "It never needs shared state", "It rejects all bursts"], "Fixed windows let a client send 100 at the end of one minute and 100 at the start of the next."),
        O("Order the rate limiter request path.", ["Identify the caller key", "Look up the bucket in shared storage", "Refill tokens by elapsed time", "Take a token or reject with 429", "Add rate-limit headers to the response"], "Atomic update in shared storage matters; do the refill and take as one step."),
        Q("The rate limiter's shared store goes down. A thoughtful answer?", ["Choose fail-open or fail-closed per route: open for ordinary traffic, closed for abuse-sensitive endpoints, and alert","Always fail closed and reject every request, so that no client can ever exceed its limit while the store is unavailable","Ignore the case in the design, since a store outage is rare enough that it is not worth discussing in an interview","Keep restarting the store indefinitely and make clients wait, because limits must be exact and requests are never dropped"], "Availability versus protection is a per-endpoint tradeoff."),
        BUG("Tap the line with the bug in this simple limiter.", ["def allow(user):", "    count = store.get(user, 0)", "    if count >= LIMIT:", "        return False", "    store[user] = count + 1", "    return True"], 1, "Read-then-write is not atomic across concurrent requests; use an atomic increment in the store."),
        NUM("The limit is 60 requests per user per minute and there are 500,000 active users. Worst-case allowed requests per second across all users?", 500000, "60 x 500,000 / 60 = 500,000 per second, which shows why you rarely size for the theoretical maximum.", null, 0, "req/s"),
        Q("Round 2 (behavioral): \"Tell me about a production bug you caused.\" Which answer lands best?", ["The impact, how you detected and mitigated it, the root cause you owned, and the process change that followed","A bug that was really caused by someone else's change, described in detail so the interviewer sees how well you investigated","A tiny typo that had no real impact on users, which shows you are careful and that your mistakes stay small","A polite refusal, explaining that you test thoroughly enough that no bug of yours has ever reached production"], "Credibility comes from honest ownership and a lasting fix."),
        M("Match each follow-up to the best one-line response.", [["Why not a relational database here?", "Access is key-based at huge scale with no joins needed"], ["What if traffic doubles?", "Scale stateless tiers horizontally and shard the hot store"], ["How do you know it works?", "Load test, define SLOs and alert on them"], ["What is your biggest risk?", "Name one concrete failure mode and how you would detect it"]], "Short, specific responses show command of the design."),
        RK("Rank these changes from lowest to highest risk of breaking existing clients.", ["Adding an optional response field", "Adding a new endpoint", "Making an optional request field required", "Changing a field's type"], "Additive changes are mostly safe. Tightening or changing existing contracts breaks callers."),
        Q("Round 3 (debugging): memory on a service climbs steadily until it restarts. Strongest investigation?", ["Capture heap profiles over time, find what grows, and match it to code paths and the deploy that started it","Double the memory limit on every instance and move on, since a bigger limit gives the service far more time before it restarts","Schedule a restart of the service every hour, so memory never reaches the limit and no investigation is needed","Remove the logging from the service, since log buffers are always what causes memory to grow steadily over time"], "A leak needs evidence of what is retained. Restarting hides it."),
        Q("Final question: \"What would you do differently on your biggest project?\" Strongest style?", ["A specific decision, why it seemed right then, what you learned, and how you apply it now","Nothing at all, because the project went perfectly and every major decision turned out to be right in hindsight","A vague wish that you had been given more time, without naming any particular decision that you would have changed","A list of mistakes made by the previous team that you inherited, and how much easier the project would have been without them"], "Hindsight with reasoning shows growth, not regret."),
        T("<p>Loop complete. If any card felt shaky, that is your study list. The goal was never to be perfect; it is to be clear, honest and structured under pressure.</p>"),
      ], "pr"),
    ],
  });

  /* ================= ML SYSTEM DESIGN ================= */
  GR.courseAddUnit("sd", {
    title: "Interview loop", sub: "The design round, end to end, out loud",
    lessons: [
      L("The questions they actually ask", "sd.framing", [
        T("<p>An ML design round rewards <b>framing before modeling</b>. The usual prompts (design a feed, fraud detection, search ranking) are commonly asked because each one forces choices about objectives, data, latency and feedback loops. Show that you design a <i>system</i>, not just a model.</p>"),
        Q("\"Design a feed ranking system.\" What is the strongest first move?", ["Clarify the objective and metric (engagement versus long-term satisfaction), scale and latency, then candidate generation, ranking and re-ranking","Pick a transformer-based ranking architecture first, since the model choice determines the features, the training data and the serving cost","List every feature you could possibly use from user, item and context data before deciding what the system is supposed to optimize","Ask how much GPU budget there is and what hardware the team already owns, since the compute limit decides whether ranking is feasible"], "The objective determines the labels, and the funnel shape handles scale."),
        Q("\"Design a fraud detection system.\" Which clarifying question matters most early?", ["How costly is a miss versus a false alarm, how delayed are labels, and must the decision be made inline?","Which gradient boosting library should we use, and does the team prefer XGBoost or LightGBM for its tabular data?","What colors and layout should the analyst dashboard use, and how often should it refresh during a shift?","How many data scientists are on the team, and could they staff a model review process every week?"], "Asymmetric costs, label delay and the latency path shape the design."),
        Q("Offline AUC went up, but the online metric did not move. What do you check first?", ["Training-serving skew and whether the offline metric matches the online goal", "Whether the model is big enough", "Whether the dashboard refreshes", "Just wait longer"], "Most such gaps come from skew, metric mismatch, or bias in the offline data."),
        Q("\"How do you get labels for a search ranking model?\"", ["Use click and engagement logs, correct for position bias, and add human relevance ratings for evaluation","Treat every click as a perfectly relevant result, because users only click on results that they actually wanted to see","Label all of the query-document pairs yourself, since only labels made by one person can be consistent enough to train on","Use no labels at all and rank by raw text similarity, since labels add noise and the similarity score never changes"], "Clicks are noisy and position-biased; human judgments anchor evaluation."),
        Q("\"How do you handle cold start for new users?\"", ["Fall back to popularity or context signals, use onboarding choices, and explore with bandits as data arrives","Show nothing to the user until they have built up enough interaction history for the model to make a confident prediction","Copy the interaction history of another user who signed up at the same time and use it as the new user's profile","Recommend random items forever, since any learned model would be biased toward the few interactions the user has made"], "Blend priors with quick learning from the first interactions."),
        Q("\"Fraud is 0.1% of transactions. How do you evaluate?\"", ["Precision-recall metrics and cost-weighted thresholds on a time-based holdout that keeps the real class ratio","Plain accuracy on a shuffled holdout, since accuracy summarizes every correct decision the model makes across all transactions","Accuracy after oversampling fraud in the test set, so that both classes are well represented in the evaluation","A comparison with a coin-flip baseline on the whole dataset, since beating chance proves that the model has learned something"], "Accuracy of 99.9% is achievable by predicting no fraud at all."),
        T("<p>Two vocabulary anchors: <b>candidate generation</b> cheaply narrows millions of items to hundreds; <b>ranking</b> spends more compute per item to order those hundreds. Say them early and you sound like you have shipped one.</p>"),
        Q("\"What is data leakage? Give an example.\"", ["Information in training that would not exist at prediction time, such as a feature computed after the outcome","A security breach in which private rows from the training database are exposed to people who should not be able to read them","Training the model for so many epochs that it memorizes the examples and fails to generalize to new data","Using so many features that the model's performance on new data becomes unreliable, even though no future data was used"], "Leakage inflates offline results and collapses in production."),
        Q("\"How do you roll out a new ranking model?\"", ["Shadow mode, then a small canary, then an A/B test with guardrail metrics, with a fast rollback path","Replace the old model at midnight when traffic is lowest, then watch dashboards the next morning and roll back if needed","Test it only offline on a held-out set, and ship it when the offline metric beats the incumbent by a clear margin","Let the team vote on whether the new model feels better after reviewing a few example rankings together"], "Increase real exposure gradually while watching guardrails."),
        Q("\"How do you monitor a model when labels arrive weeks late?\"", ["Track input and prediction drift and proxy metrics now, then compute true performance as labels land","Wait until the labels arrive before monitoring anything, since drift metrics without ground truth cannot be interpreted at all","Monitor only server CPU, memory and request latency, since those show whether the service is up and healthy","Retrain on a fixed schedule and never check the results, since fresh data keeps the model current without extra monitoring"], "Delayed truth means you need leading indicators."),
        M("Match each component to its purpose.", [["Feature store", "Same feature definitions online and offline"], ["Model registry", "Versioned model artifacts and their metadata"], ["Vector index", "Approximate nearest-neighbor retrieval of embeddings"], ["Label pipeline", "Joins outcomes back to logged predictions"]], "Four pieces that show up in nearly every design."),
        BN("Batch feature or real-time feature?", ["Batch (computed on a schedule)", "Real-time (computed at request or streaming)"], [["User's 30-day purchase count", 0], ["Item embeddings refreshed nightly", 0], ["Account age", 0], ["Clicks in the last five minutes", 1], ["Current cart contents", 1], ["Device fingerprint at request time", 1]], "Match freshness to how quickly the signal changes and how much staleness hurts."),
        RK("Rank these features from freshest to stalest.", ["Request-time context (device, location)", "Streaming aggregate over the last 5 minutes", "Hourly batch aggregate", "Nightly batch aggregate"], "Fresher features cost more infrastructure; use them where freshness changes the decision."),
        NUM("A feed is loaded 86.4 million times a day, evenly. Average QPS?", 1000, "86,400,000 / 86,400 seconds = 1,000 requests per second.", null, 0, "QPS"),
        NUM("An index stores 100 million item embeddings of 128 dimensions as 4-byte floats. How many GB (decimal)?", 51.2, "100,000,000 x 128 x 4 bytes = 51.2 billion bytes = 51.2 GB, before index overhead.", null, 0.1, "GB"),
        BUG("This pipeline leaks information from the test set. Tap the line.", ["df = load_events()", "scaler.fit(df[FEATURES])", "X = scaler.transform(df[FEATURES])", "X_tr, X_te, y_tr, y_te = split(X, df.label)", "model.fit(X_tr, y_tr)"], 1, "Fitting the scaler on all rows lets test statistics influence training. Split first, then fit on training data only."),
        BUG("The goal is to predict next-week behavior. Tap the flawed line.", ["df = load_user_weeks()", "train = df.sample(frac=0.8, random_state=0)", "test = df.drop(train.index)", "model.fit(train[FEATURES], train.label)"], 1, "A random split mixes future and past weeks. Use a time-based split so the test period is strictly after training."),
        F("Fill in the right metric names.", "Ranking quality with position discount: ___@k\nImbalanced classification curve: ___", ["NDCG", "precision-recall"], ["accuracy", "RMSE", "ROC area"], "NDCG rewards putting the best items first. Precision-recall focuses on the rare positive class."),
        Q("\"Why use a two-stage (retrieve then rank) architecture?\"", ["Cheap recall over millions of items, then expensive precision over a few hundred","It is simpler to build, since each stage can be a small independent model that is trained without any shared features","Rankers cannot handle fewer than a million items, so a retrieval stage is needed to reach the minimum size","It removes the need for features, because retrieval already encodes everything the ranker would otherwise have to learn"], "Compute budget makes scoring everything with the heavy model impossible."),
        Q("\"How would you evaluate a search ranking change offline?\"", ["Compute NDCG on human-rated or click-derived judgments, then confirm with an online experiment","Look at the results for a handful of example queries, and ship if the top results look better to the team","Measure the size of the new model and its training loss, since a smaller model with lower loss is always an improvement","Check the training loss only and ship if it fell, because the loss is the same quantity that the ranking metric tracks"], "Offline metrics screen candidates; online experiments decide."),
        Q("\"How do you pick the decision threshold for a fraud model?\"", ["From the cost of false positives versus missed fraud, on a recent holdout, and revisit as the mix changes", "Always 0.5", "Whatever gives the highest accuracy", "The same as the last model"], "Thresholds are a business decision informed by costs and capacity."),
        Q("A recommender only learns from what it already shows. What risk is this?", ["A feedback loop: the model reinforces its own exposure, so you need exploration and debiasing", "Overfitting to the validation set", "Underfitting", "Label leakage from the future"], "Logged data is selected by the previous policy."),
        O("Order the funnel of a recommendation request.", ["Fetch user context and features", "Retrieve candidates from several sources", "Filter ineligible items", "Score with the ranking model", "Re-rank for diversity and policy", "Log impressions for training"], "Cheap steps first, expensive scoring on fewer items, logging closes the loop."),
        T("<p>Strong answers always <b>close the loop</b>: what you log, how it becomes tomorrow's training data, and how you will notice if it breaks. Mention that, and your design stops being a diagram and becomes a system.</p>"),
      ], "learn"),

      L("Say it out loud", "sd.framing", [
        T("<p>Most of the grade is structure. Use the same <b>skeleton</b> every time so the interviewer always knows where you are, and you never forget monitoring because you ran out of clock.</p>"),
        O("Order the ML design round skeleton.", ["Clarify the problem and goal", "Define business and ML metrics", "State constraints and scale", "Data and labels", "Features", "Model and training", "Serving architecture", "Offline and online evaluation", "Monitoring and rollout", "Risks and iteration"], "A skeleton you can recite means you spend your thinking on the content."),
        O("Order a feed ranking walk-through.", ["Candidate generation from several sources", "Pre-filtering for eligibility and safety", "Ranking model scoring a few hundred", "Re-ranking for diversity and policy", "Logging impressions and outcomes"], "The funnel narrows as compute per item increases."),
        O("Order a fraud detection walk-through.", ["Pin down loss costs and the latency budget", "Build labels, accounting for chargeback delay", "Engineer velocity, device and graph features", "Train with a time-based split", "Choose the threshold from a cost curve", "Route the gray zone to review and monitor drift"], "Costs and labels come before models."),
        RK("Rank these from least to most realistic evidence a model is good.", ["Offline AUC on a random split", "Offline metric on a time-based holdout", "Shadow-mode comparison on live traffic", "A/B test on the business metric"], "Each step is closer to the real deployment conditions."),
        RK("Rank rollout stages from least to most user exposure.", ["Shadow mode", "Canary at 1%", "A/B test at 50%", "Full launch"], "Shadow users see nothing; a full launch exposes everyone."),
        M("Match each question to the key point a strong answer must hit.", [["Why two-stage ranking?", "Cheap recall over millions, expensive precision over hundreds"], ["Why a time-based split?", "Avoids future leakage and mirrors deployment"], ["Why a feature store?", "One definition of each feature for training and serving"], ["Why shadow deployment?", "Test on live traffic with no user impact"]], "Each answer is one sentence of reasoning."),
        M("Match each metric to what it measures.", [["Precision@k", "Fraction of the top k that is relevant"], ["Recall@k", "Fraction of all relevant items found in the top k"], ["NDCG", "Ranking quality with position-discounted graded gain"], ["Log loss", "Probability quality, punishing confident mistakes"]], "Know which metric answers which question."),
        BN("Where does each topic belong in your answer?", ["Data and labels", "Serving", "Monitoring"], [["Chargeback delay", 0], ["Point-in-time correct joins", 0], ["p99 latency budget", 1], ["Fallback when the model times out", 1], ["Population stability index", 2], ["Alert on prediction-score drift", 2]], "Slotting topics into the skeleton keeps you from rambling."),
        Q("The interviewer asks \"how would you evaluate this?\" Strongest structure?", ["Offline metric tied to the objective, then an online experiment with guardrails and a stated duration","\"I'd check accuracy on the test set and report it, since accuracy summarizes how often the model is right.\"","\"I'd look at some example outputs by hand and see whether they seem reasonable to me.\"","\"The training loss goes down steadily, so the model is learning and it works.\""], "Evaluation has two layers and a guardrail story."),
        Q("Which sentence states an ML tradeoff well?", ["\"A bigger model gains about a point of recall for 40 ms, so I'd test whether latency costs engagement.\"","\"A bigger model is better, so I'd use the biggest one the budget allows and accept the extra latency.\"","\"We should use deep learning here, because it beats classical methods on nearly every ranking problem.\"","\"It depends on the data and the users, so there is no way to say which model is the right choice.\""], "Quantify both sides and name how you would decide."),
        Q("You are 20 minutes in and have not mentioned monitoring. What do you do?", ["Pause the detail, say you want to reserve time for monitoring and rollout, and cover them concisely","Skip it, since monitoring is an optional extra and the interviewer is mostly interested in the model and the data","Mention it in the last ten seconds as a list of tools, so you technically covered it before time ran out","Ask the interviewer whether they care about monitoring, and only cover it if they say that it matters to them"], "Production concerns are a core signal; budget time for them."),
        Q("An interviewer asks \"what is the biggest risk in your design?\" Strongest answer?", ["One concrete risk (such as feedback loops or label delay), how you would detect it, and your mitigation","\"There are no big risks, because the design uses proven components and I have covered failure handling for each of them.\"","\"The model might be wrong sometimes, so I would add a manual review step for every prediction it makes.\"","\"Hardware failures could take servers down, so I would run the service across several machines and zones.\""], "Specific risk, detection, mitigation."),
        NUM("Latency budget is 100 ms. Network takes 20, feature fetch 25, and business rules 5. How many ms remain for model inference?", 50, "100 - 20 - 25 - 5 = 50 ms.", null, 0, "ms"),
        NUM("Peak traffic is 3,000 requests per second and each request scores 200 candidates. How many item scores per second?", 600000, "3,000 x 200 = 600,000 scores per second, which is why candidate counts matter.", null, 0, "scores/s"),
        NUM("A funnel retrieves 1,000 candidates from 10 million items. What fraction of the catalog is that, in percent?", 0.01, "1,000 / 10,000,000 = 0.0001 = 0.01%.", null, 0.001, "%"),
        F("Complete the metric definitions.", "precision = TP / (TP + ___)\nrecall = TP / (TP + ___)", ["FP", "FN"], ["TN", "TP"], "Precision penalizes false alarms; recall penalizes misses."),
        Q("How should you describe the training data window for a fraud model?", ["Recent, time-ordered data with labels matured past the chargeback window, evaluated on a later holdout","All data ever collected, shuffled together, so that every pattern in the full history appears in each split","Only last night's data, since the freshest transactions are the best predictor of what fraudsters will do next","Whatever is easiest to load from the warehouse, since the exact window matters less than the amount of training data"], "Respect time and label maturity."),
        Q("What is the best answer to \"why not just use a rules engine?\"", ["Rules are fast and explainable for known patterns, a model catches subtle ones; use both, rules as guardrails","Rules never work against fraud, because fraudsters read the rules and adapt, so a model should always replace them entirely","Models never need rules, because a well-trained model already encodes every policy and every hard business limit","Rules are always cheaper to build and maintain, so a model is only worth adding after the rules have been exhausted"], "Hybrid designs are the norm in fraud."),
        Q("What phrase best signals that you are explicitly handling a feedback loop?", ["\"I'll reserve a small exploration slice and log propensities so we can debias.\"","\"We'll retrain nightly on the latest logs, so the model keeps up with whatever the users are doing now.\"","\"We will trust the clicks as the label, since users click on what they find most relevant.\"","\"The model will fix itself over time, because it keeps learning from the data it produces.\""], "Exploration plus logging propensities supports unbiased learning."),
        BUG("Tap the line that makes this A/B assignment wrong for a per-user experiment.", ["def bucket(request):", "    h = hash(request.request_id)", "    return 'treatment' if h % 100 < 50 else 'control'", "", "# used on every page load"], 1, "Hashing the request ID gives the same user different arms on different loads. Hash the user ID, with an experiment salt."),
        Q("What should you say about a model that is accurate but unexplainable in a regulated lending context?", ["Weigh accuracy against regulatory explanation needs, possibly using an interpretable model or explanation tooling","Accuracy always wins, since a model that predicts better is fairer to applicants and regulators should accept the results","Explainability is irrelevant here, because regulators only audit the outcomes and never ask how the model reached them","Use a coin flip for the borderline cases, because randomness cannot be accused of bias against a protected group"], "Constraints from the domain can override raw accuracy."),
        O("Order a monitoring answer.", ["Data quality and schema checks on inputs", "Feature and prediction distribution drift", "Proxy business metrics", "True model performance as labels arrive", "Alerting and a rollback runbook"], "Earliest signals first, truth last, action always."),
        Q("What is a good way to end a design answer?", ["Recap the flow, the key tradeoff and what you would build first", "Say \"that's everything\"", "Apologize for gaps", "Ask whether it was good"], "A short recap shows control of the design."),
        T("<p>Practice move: set a 35-minute timer, pick a prompt, and speak the skeleton out loud with a whiteboard. Time-box each step: 5 minutes of framing is worth more than 5 extra minutes of model talk.</p>"),
      ], "learn"),

      L("Follow-ups they throw at you", "sd.monitoring", [
        T("<p>After your first design, the interviewer picks the weakest joint and pulls. These are commonly asked follow-ups; the right instinct is usually <b>data and measurement</b> before more modeling.</p>"),
        Q("\"Your labels arrive 30 days late. How do you monitor?\"", ["Watch input and prediction drift and proxy metrics now, and score true performance as labels mature","Stop monitoring for 30 days and then review everything at once, since drift signals are not reliable until labels are in","Use only latency and error-rate metrics, since they are the only signals available before ground truth arrives","Retrain daily on the newest data and assume it is fine, since a freshly trained model cannot have drifted"], "Leading indicators bridge the gap until truth arrives."),
        Q("\"Clicks are biased by position. How do you fix training?\"", ["Log the position, use inverse propensity weighting or a position-aware model, and randomize a small slice to estimate bias", "Ignore it", "Remove the top results", "Train only on position 1"], "Higher slots get more clicks regardless of quality."),
        Q("\"Serving and training disagree on a feature value.\" What is the structural fix?", ["Compute features through one shared definition and log the served features for training", "Retrain more often", "Add more features", "Raise the learning rate"], "Skew is a pipeline problem, not a modeling problem."),
        Q("\"The new model is 2% better offline but 40 ms slower.\"", ["Measure whether the latency hurts engagement, and consider distillation or a cascade to keep the gain cheaper","Ship it right away, since a two percent accuracy gain is worth more than a small delay that most users will not notice","Reject it at once, since any added latency is a regression and speed always beats a small gain in accuracy","Average its scores with the old model's scores and ship the blend, which splits the gain and the delay evenly"], "Latency is a product metric; test the net effect."),
        Q("\"How long do you run the A/B test?\"", ["At least a full weekly cycle and long enough to hit the sample size from a power calculation", "One hour", "Until the result looks significant", "Exactly one day"], "Weekly seasonality and pre-computed power prevent false calls."),
        Q("\"An experiment shows a big lift in week one that fades.\" Likely explanation?", ["A novelty effect, so extend the test and examine returning-user cohorts", "A bug in the arithmetic", "The model learned too much", "Random noise always"], "New experiences attract attention that decays."),
        Q("\"What is the difference between covariate shift and concept drift?\"", ["Covariate shift changes the input distribution; concept drift changes how inputs relate to the label","They are the same thing under two names, and both mean that the feature distribution has changed since training","Concept drift only happens in image models, where the pixels stay the same but the meaning of the labels changes","Covariate shift only affects the labels, so the inputs keep the same distribution and the label rates move"], "One is P(x), the other is P(y given x)."),
        M("Match each failure to its definition.", [["Covariate shift", "The input distribution changes"], ["Label shift", "The outcome rate changes"], ["Concept drift", "The input-to-outcome relationship changes"], ["Training-serving skew", "Features computed differently in training and production"]], "Different causes need different fixes."),
        BN("Which kind of drift is each example?", ["Covariate shift", "Concept drift"], [["A new market brings younger users", 0], ["A camera upgrade changes image brightness", 0], ["A new app version changes input formats", 0], ["Fraudsters change tactics after detection", 1], ["A recession changes what normal spending predicts", 1], ["A policy change alters what counts as spam", 1]], "Inputs moving versus meaning changing."),
        NUM("A fraud model flags 200 of 1,000,000 transactions; 150 of those are fraud. Precision, in percent?", 75, "150 / 200 = 75%.", null, 0, "%"),
        NUM("Same model: there are 300 fraudulent transactions in total. Recall, in percent?", 50, "150 caught of 300 = 50%.", null, 0, "%"),
        NUM("Use n = 16 p(1-p) / d^2 per group. Baseline conversion is 10% and you want to detect a 1-point absolute change. Users per group?", 14400, "16 x 0.1 x 0.9 / 0.0001 = 14,400 per group (about 80% power, 5% significance).", null, 100, "users"),
        NUM("A flagged transaction costs $5 in review if legitimate; a missed fraud costs $200. Flag when fraud probability exceeds what percent (one decimal)?", 2.4, "Flag when p x 200 > (1 - p) x 5, so p > 5 / 205, about 2.4%.", null, 0.05, "%"),
        NUM("20 million items with 256-dim embeddings stored in 2-byte floats. How many GB (decimal)?", 10.24, "20,000,000 x 256 x 2 = 10.24 billion bytes = 10.24 GB.", null, 0.01, "GB"),
        BUG("Tap the line that breaks a per-user experiment.", ["def assign(user_id, request_id):", "    key = request_id", "    return 'B' if hash(key) % 2 else 'A'"], 1, "Keying on the request means one user flips between arms. Use the user ID plus an experiment salt."),
        BUG("This training set is supposed to predict tomorrow's churn. Tap the leaking line.", ["features = build_features(user, as_of=today)", "features['days_since_cancel'] = days_since(user.cancel_date)", "label = user.churned_next_day", "return features, label"], 1, "Cancel date is only known after churning, so the feature encodes the answer. Features must exist before the prediction time."),
        RK("Rank these monitoring signals from earliest to latest available.", ["Input schema and null-rate checks", "Feature distribution drift", "Prediction score distribution", "Proxy engagement metrics", "True labeled performance"], "Cheap checks fire first; truth arrives last."),
        O("Order the response to a model-quality alert.", ["Confirm the alert and its scope", "Check upstream data freshness and pipelines", "Compare feature distributions to baseline", "Roll back to the last good model if users are harmed", "Root-cause, fix and add a monitor"], "Rule out data problems before blaming the model."),
        F("Fill in the exploration idea.", "To collect unbiased feedback, show a small random slice and log the ___ of each shown item, so it can be corrected for later with ___ weighting.", ["propensity", "inverse propensity"], ["accuracy", "gradient", "TF-IDF"], "Known show-probabilities allow reweighting toward the unbiased distribution."),
        Q("\"How often do you retrain?\"", ["Set by how fast the data drifts and what staleness costs, triggered by drift metrics, not a fixed guess","Daily, always, since a fixed daily schedule is simple to operate and guarantees that the model never gets stale","Never, since a model that was validated before launch should not be changed without a full new round of review","Whenever someone on the team remembers or notices a drop in a dashboard, with no schedule or trigger written down"], "Retraining cadence is empirical."),
        Q("\"The model's score distribution suddenly shifts but inputs look normal.\" First suspect?", ["A bad deployment or pipeline change affecting a feature or model version", "The users became unpredictable", "Gradient descent drift", "Cosmic rays"], "Sudden shifts usually have mechanical causes."),
        Q("\"How do you handle a new item with no interaction history?\"", ["Use content features and metadata, plus exploration so it gets measured quickly", "Never show it", "Show it first on every page", "Copy a popular item's score"], "Content-based signals cover cold start while interaction data accumulates."),
        Q("\"Offline evaluation says model B is better but A/B says no difference.\"", ["Trust the experiment, and investigate metric mismatch or selection bias in the offline data","Trust the offline number, since it was measured on far more data than the experiment could collect in its shorter window","Run the offline evaluation again with a different random split, and ship if model B wins again on the second try","Average the two results and ship if the blend is positive, since each measurement captures part of the true effect"], "Online results reflect real behavior."),
        Q("\"How do you keep a fraud model fair and compliant?\"", ["Audit performance across groups, limit sensitive proxies, keep explanations and review paths, and document decisions","Remove all the data that describes people from training, since a model with no personal data cannot treat anyone unfairly","Skip human review because the model is automated, so decisions are consistent and cannot be influenced by bias","Rely on the vendor's fairness statement, since they already tested the model and documented it for their customers"], "Compliance is a design requirement."),
        T("<p>Take a breath: the best follow-up answer is usually \"I'd measure that first.\" It is not dodging; it is the job.</p>"),
      ], "drill"),

      L("Mock loop", "sd.serving", [
        T("<p>Mock round: <b>design search ranking for a marketplace</b>. Answer each card as the next beat of the conversation.</p>"),
        Q("Beat 1: what do you establish first?", ["The goal (find and buy the right item), the metric (for example conversion and NDCG), scale, and latency target","Which embedding model to use for query and item text, and whether to fine-tune it on the catalog before anything else","The logging format and schema for impressions and clicks, so that every later step has consistent data to work with","The cloud provider and region, since latency and cost targets depend on where the ranking service will be deployed"], "Goal, metric and constraints."),
        RK("Beat 2: rank these as candidate sources by how essential they are for recall on a keyword search.", ["Inverted index with lexical matching", "Embedding-based nearest neighbors", "Popular items for the category", "Recently viewed by the user"], "Lexical matching is the backbone; embeddings add semantic recall; popularity and personal history supplement."),
        M("Beat 3: match each data signal to its use.", [["Click and purchase logs", "Training labels with debiasing"], ["Query text", "Retrieval and relevance features"], ["Item catalog attributes", "Content features and cold-start handling"], ["Human relevance ratings", "Evaluation and calibration"]], "Each source plays a different role."),
        Q("Beat 4: clicks on the top result are 3x higher than on position 5 for equal quality. What do you do?", ["Treat position as a bias: log it, weight or model it, and drop it at serving", "Ignore it", "Treat all clicks as equal relevance", "Delete top-position data"], "Position bias distorts labels."),
        NUM("Beat 5: ranking scores 300 candidates per query in 30 ms total. At 2,000 queries per second, how many scores per second?", 600000, "300 x 2,000 = 600,000 scores per second.", null, 0, "scores/s"),
        Q("Beat 6: the ranker times out under load. Best graceful degradation?", ["Fall back to a cached or lightweight ranker so results still appear","Return a clear error page and ask users to retry shortly, so no one sees degraded or inconsistent results","Return random results from the catalog so that the page still renders within the latency budget","Retry the ranker call until it succeeds, since the timeout is a temporary condition that will pass"], "Degrade quality before availability."),
        BUG("Beat 7: tap the line that leaks the future into this relevance model.", ["df = load_query_logs()", "df['ctr_30d'] = df.groupby('item_id').clicked.transform('mean')", "train = df[df.day < cutoff]", "test = df[df.day >= cutoff]"], 1, "The item click rate is computed across all days, including the test period. Compute it only from data before each example's timestamp."),
        Q("Beat 8: what is your launch plan?", ["Shadow comparison, a 1% canary with guardrails on latency and conversion, then a 50/50 A/B for at least a full week","Launch to everyone at once and watch the dashboards closely, then roll back if conversion or latency starts to drop","Launch only if offline NDCG rises, and skip online testing, since the offline metric already predicts conversion well enough","Launch on Friday evening when traffic is lowest, so that any problems affect the fewest users before the weekend ends"], "Gradual exposure with guardrails."),
        Q("Beat 9: after launch, conversions drop for one category only. First step?", ["Slice metrics by category and check feature and data freshness for that slice","Retrain the whole model on the latest data right away, since a drop in one category means that the model has gone stale","Ignore it for now, since one category is a small share of revenue and will likely recover without any changes","Revert the entire launch at once without looking at any data, since reverting is cheaper than finding the cause"], "Localize before acting."),
        O("Beat 10: order the final recap.", ["Goal and metric", "Retrieval and ranking funnel", "Data and labels with debiasing", "Serving latency and fallbacks", "Rollout and monitoring"], "A recap in skeleton order."),
        Q("Beat 11: \"What would you build first?\"", ["A simple lexical retrieval plus gradient-boosted ranker with logging, to get a baseline and data flowing","The most advanced neural architecture you can justify, such as a large transformer cross-encoder for ranking and retrieval","A custom vector database tuned for the catalog, so that retrieval is fast enough before any ranking exists","A polished dashboard that shows every metric to stakeholders, so that progress on the launch is visible to everyone"], "Baseline and data flywheel first."),
        T("<p>That is a full round. If you hit goal, funnel, data, serving, rollout and monitoring in order, you have a skeleton interviewers love.</p>"),
      ], "pr"),
    ],
  });

  /* ================= INVESTING AND TRADING ================= */
  GR.courseAddUnit("fin", {
    title: "Interview loop", sub: "Mental math, expected value and market sense, out loud",
    lessons: [
      L("The questions they actually ask", "fin.markets", [
        T("<p>Quant, trading and finance interviews lean on a small set of staples: <b>mental math</b>, <b>expected value</b>, <b>compounding</b>, <b>risk-adjusted return</b>, <b>bond and option intuition</b>, and <b>why backtests lie</b>. Everything here is educational; none of it is advice about what to do with your own money.</p>"),
        Q("\"You pay $10 to roll a fair die and receive the face value in dollars. Do you play?\"", ["No: the expected payout is $3.50, so the expected loss is $6.50 per play","Yes, because any single roll could pay up to $6, and a bet with an upside is always worth taking at fair odds","Yes, since the average face value of a die is $10, so the expected payout equals the price","It depends on how risk-tolerant you are, since a fair die has no expected value to compare with the price"], "EV of a fair die is (1+2+3+4+5+6)/6 = 3.5, below the price."),
        Q("\"About how long for money to double at 6% a year?\"", ["About 12 years, from the rule of 72", "About 6 years", "About 20 years", "About 16 years"], "72 / 6 = 12. It is a quick approximation, accurate enough for rates in the middle range."),
        Q("\"Rates rise. What happens to the price of an existing fixed-rate bond?\"", ["It falls, because its fixed coupons are discounted at a higher rate", "It rises, because it pays more", "It does not change", "It becomes worthless"], "Price is the present value of fixed cash flows, and a higher discount rate shrinks it."),
        Q("\"What does the Sharpe ratio tell you?\"", ["Excess return over the risk-free rate per unit of volatility", "Total return divided by the number of trades", "The probability of losing money", "The worst drawdown"], "Sharpe = (return - risk-free) / standard deviation."),
        T("<p>Interview tip: when a number is asked for, say your <b>approach</b> before the digit. \"EV is probability times payoff, summed\" buys you time and credit even if your arithmetic slips.</p>"),
        Q("\"Why do backtests lie?\" Strongest answer?", ["Overfitting to many tries, lookahead and survivorship bias, and ignoring costs, slippage and capacity","They are just wrong because the historical data is always corrupted by errors that no amount of cleaning will remove","Markets are random, so any pattern found in the past can never work in the future under any circumstances at all","Computers are inaccurate at the scale of financial data, so rounding errors accumulate until the results are meaningless"], "A backtest is a model of the past with many ways to flatter itself."),
        Q("\"A market maker quotes 99.90 bid, 100.10 ask. How does it make money?\"", ["It earns the spread on two-sided flow while managing inventory and the risk of trading against informed traders","It predicts every price move ahead of time and takes the right side of each trade, which lets it avoid inventory entirely","It charges a commission on every trade and has no exposure to price changes, so the quoted spread is just a fee","It holds the stock for years and profits when the price rises, using the bid and ask only to enter and exit positions"], "The spread pays for inventory risk and adverse selection."),
        Q("\"Is a 10% annual return good?\"", ["It depends on the risk taken, the horizon, and the benchmark", "Yes, always", "No, never", "Only if it is above 15%"], "Returns mean little without risk and comparison."),
        Q("\"Why does diversification help?\"", ["Imperfectly correlated assets offset each other, reducing volatility without cutting expected return in proportion","It guarantees a profit, because losses on one holding are always covered by gains on another across any market regime","It removes market risk entirely, since holding enough different assets cancels out every common source of movement","It raises expected return by itself, because adding more holdings always increases the average return of the portfolio"], "Diversification reduces idiosyncratic risk; market-wide risk remains."),
        NUM("Mental math: 17 x 23 = ?", 391, "17 x 20 = 340 and 17 x 3 = 51, so 391.", null, 0),
        NUM("Two fair dice are rolled. Probability the sum is 7, in percent (one decimal)?", 16.7, "6 of 36 outcomes sum to 7, which is 1/6, about 16.7%.", null, 0.05, "%"),
        NUM("$1,000 grows at 7% per year, compounded annually, for 10 years. Nearest dollar?", 1967, "1.07^10 is about 1.9672, so $1,967.", null, 1, "$"),
        M("Match each term to its meaning.", [["Sharpe ratio", "Excess return per unit of total volatility"], ["Beta", "Sensitivity to the market's moves"], ["Max drawdown", "Worst peak-to-trough loss"], ["Alpha", "Return beyond what the risk taken explains"]], "Four risk and performance terms you must define crisply."),
        BN("Is it a backtest flaw or a sound practice?", ["Backtest flaw", "Sound practice"], [["Testing 2005 using today's index members only", 0], ["Ignoring fees and slippage", 0], ["Tuning 200 parameters on the same data you report", 0], ["Walk-forward testing", 1], ["Holding out an untouched final period", 1], ["Reporting how many variants were tried", 1]], "Survivorship, costs and data snooping are the classic sins."),
        RK("Rank these from lowest to highest typical volatility.", ["3-month Treasury bill", "10-year Treasury bond", "Diversified stock index", "A single stock"], "Short government paper barely moves; single names carry company-specific risk on top of market risk."),
        F("Fill in the option payoffs at expiry.", "Call payoff = max(S - ___, 0)\nPut payoff = max(K - ___, 0)", ["K", "S"], ["r", "T", "sigma"], "S is the final price of the underlying and K is the strike."),
        BUG("This backtest cheats. Tap the line.", ["returns = prices.pct_change()", "signal = (returns > 0).astype(int)", "strategy = signal * returns", "print(strategy.mean())"], 2, "The signal uses today's return to hold a position earning today's return, which is lookahead. Shift the signal by one day first."),
        Q("\"Flip a fair coin until you get heads. Expected number of flips?\"", ["2", "1", "4", "3"], "The expected value is 1/p = 1/0.5 = 2."),
        Q("\"Stock A returns +50% then -50%. Where does an investor end up?\"", ["25% below the start, since 1.5 x 0.5 = 0.75", "Back at the start", "25% above the start", "50% below the start"], "Percentages compound multiplicatively, which is volatility drag."),
        Q("\"What is the payoff of a long call at expiry?\"", ["max(S - K, 0), with the loss limited to the premium paid","Unlimited loss, since a call buyer must cover any rise in the price of the underlying above the strike","A fixed coupon paid on a schedule until expiry, regardless of where the underlying price finishes","S - K always, even if negative, with no premium paid, so the position can lose more than it cost"], "A call buyer has limited downside and open-ended upside."),
        Q("\"What is the difference between a bond's coupon and its yield?\"", ["The coupon is the fixed payment on face value; the yield is the return implied by today's price","They are always equal, because a bond's yield is defined as its coupon divided by its original face value","Yield is paid out annually while the coupon is paid monthly, so they differ only in the payment schedule","The coupon is the bond's market price, while the yield is the amount of principal returned at maturity"], "Price moves, so the same coupon implies different yields."),
        Q("\"Why can't a market maker just quote very tight spreads forever?\"", ["Informed traders would pick them off, and tight spreads may not cover inventory and operating costs","Regulations forbid quotes tighter than a minimum spread, so every market maker must widen quotes to the same level","Exchanges close at night, so the spread must be wide enough to cover the overnight price gap each day","Spreads cannot be changed once posted, so a market maker has to choose one level and keep it for the whole day"], "Adverse selection is the key concept."),
        Q("\"Is a high Sharpe in a backtest trustworthy?\"", ["Not by itself: check trial count, costs, out-of-sample results and capacity","Yes, since a Sharpe above 1 over a long backtest is real evidence that the strategy has an edge in live trading","Only if the backtest is long, because a long enough history removes the overfitting that comes from trying many ideas","Yes, it is a mathematical fact about the past returns, and the past return series is exactly what a backtest measures"], "Search over many strategies inflates the best observed Sharpe."),
        T("<p>Next session is how to <b>say</b> these answers. A correct answer that is mumbled scores lower than a mostly correct answer that is structured.</p>"),
      ], "learn"),

      L("Say it out loud", "fin.valuation", [
        T("<p>Brain-teasers are tests of <b>process</b>. State assumptions, pick the simplest model, compute, sanity check, and add a caveat. Interviewers will forgive an arithmetic slip; they will not forgive silence.</p>"),
        O("Order a probability brain-teaser answer.", ["Restate the problem and assumptions (fair, independent)", "Pick the simplest model", "Set up the outcomes and probabilities", "Compute, and say each step", "Sanity check against bounds", "State the answer and one caveat"], "Structure first; the number last."),
        O("Order a bond pricing explanation.", ["A bond is a stream of fixed cash flows", "Price is the present value of those flows", "Discount at the market yield", "Higher yield means lower price", "Longer maturity means more sensitivity (duration)"], "Present value is the entire story."),
        O("Order an option payoff walk-through.", ["Identify call or put, long or short", "Write the payoff at expiry using the strike", "Subtract (or add) the premium for profit", "Find the breakeven price", "State max loss and max gain"], "Payoff, premium, breakeven, extremes."),
        O("Order a market-making explanation.", ["Quote a bid and an ask around fair value", "Earn the spread when both sides trade", "Track inventory as flow becomes one-sided", "Skew quotes to move inventory back", "Widen or pull quotes when informed flow is suspected"], "Spread income versus inventory and adverse selection."),
        RK("Rank these bets from best to worst expected value.", ["Win $12 or lose $8 on a fair coin", "Pay $3 to receive a fair die roll's face value", "Pay $3.50 to receive a fair die roll's face value", "Pay $4 to receive a fair die roll's face value"], "EVs are +2, +0.5, 0 and -0.5 dollars."),
        M("Match each question to the key point a strong answer hits.", [["What is the expected value of a bet?", "Probability-weighted sum of outcomes, compared with the price"], ["Why do bond prices fall when yields rise?", "Fixed coupons are discounted at a higher rate"], ["Why distrust a backtested Sharpe of 3?", "Overfitting, costs and multiple tries inflate it"], ["Why can't a market maker quote tight forever?", "Informed traders pick them off"]], "Concept first, number second."),
        M("Match each option position to its risk profile.", [["Long call", "Loss capped at the premium, gain unlimited"], ["Long put", "Loss capped at the premium, gain up to strike minus premium"], ["Short uncovered call", "Gain capped at the premium, loss unlimited"], ["Covered call", "Gives up upside above the strike for premium income"]], "Know the shape of each payoff."),
        BN("Say it, or skip it, in a brain-teaser answer?", ["Say it", "Skip it"], [["\"I'll assume the coin is fair and flips are independent.\"", 0], ["\"EV is probability times payoff, summed.\"", 0], ["\"Sanity check: the answer must be between 1 and 6.\"", 0], ["Silence for 90 seconds, then a number", 1], ["\"I just remember the answer.\"", 1], ["A wild guess presented as certain", 1]], "Assumptions, method and checks demonstrate how you think."),
        Q("The interviewer says \"you win $1 per heads, flip until tails.\" Your first sentence?", ["\"Let me confirm: fair coin, $1 per heads before the first tail, and I'm computing expected payout.\"","\"The answer is 2, since the game lasts two flips on average and each flip pays one dollar.\"","\"I need a calculator or some paper before I can start, so could we pause while I set up?\"","\"That's easy, it is the classic geometric series, so I'll just write down the answer and move on.\""], "Restating the setup catches misunderstandings early."),
        Q("You realize mid-answer that you made an arithmetic error. Best move?", ["Say so, correct it, and carry on", "Hope no one noticed", "Stop and refuse to continue", "Change the question"], "Calm self-correction is a positive signal."),
        Q("You do not know the answer to a pricing question. Best move?", ["Break it down, say what you know, bound the answer, and give a reasoned estimate","Say \"I don't know\" and stop there, since a guess that turns out wrong looks worse than an honest admission","Guess a number at random and state it confidently, since the interviewer mostly wants to see how decisive you are","Change the subject to a topic you know well, so the conversation shifts to your strengths before time runs out"], "Estimation under uncertainty is the job."),
        NUM("Mental math: 48 x 25 = ?", 1200, "48 x 100 / 4 = 4,800 / 4 = 1,200.", null, 0),
        NUM("A 15% tip on a $64 bill is how many dollars?", 9.6, "10% is 6.40 and 5% is 3.20, so 9.60.", null, 0.01, "$"),
        NUM("Mental math: 99 x 99 = ?", 9801, "(100 - 1)^2 = 10,000 - 200 + 1 = 9,801.", null, 0),
        F("Fill in the rule of thumb.", "At 8% growth, doubling takes about 72 / ___ = 9 years.", ["8"], ["72", "100", "9"], "The rule of 72 divides 72 by the rate in percent."),
        Q("How should you phrase a valuation answer when information is missing?", ["\"Assuming 5% growth and an 8% discount rate, the value is X; here is how it changes if those move.\"","\"It is worth whatever the market says it is worth, since the price is the only valuation that clears trades.\"","\"I cannot answer without the full financial statements, so could you provide them before I continue?\"","\"It is clearly cheap compared with where it traded last year, so I would call it undervalued right now.\""], "State assumptions and sensitivity."),
        Q("A strong way to explain the Sharpe ratio in one breath?", ["\"Return above cash for each unit of volatility, so it lets me compare strategies with different risk.\"","\"Total return divided by the number of trades, so it shows how much a strategy earns on an average trade.\"","\"How often a strategy wins, so a higher Sharpe simply means a higher percentage of profitable trades.\"","\"The biggest loss a strategy has had, so it measures the worst peak-to-trough drop in the account.\""], "Define, then say why it is useful."),
        Q("Explaining why options have time value, the strongest short answer?", ["Until expiry there is still a chance the underlying moves in your favor, and you have limited downside","Because options are old contracts and carry a premium for age, which shrinks as the contract gets closer to expiry","Because brokers charge a fee for the time the position is open, and the fee is built into the quoted price","It has no basis, since an option is worth exactly its intrinsic value and nothing more at any time before expiry"], "Optionality has value before expiry."),
        Q("When asked to price a bet, which sanity check is most useful?", ["Compare against the best and worst possible payoffs", "Check that the answer is an integer", "Check that it is positive", "Round to ten"], "The expected value must lie between the extremes."),
        BUG("Tap the line with the compounding bug.", ["total = 1.0", "for r in returns:", "    total += r", "print(total - 1)"], 2, "Returns compound multiplicatively: total *= 1 + r."),
        Q("The interviewer says \"is this stock cheap?\" Best structured start?", ["Ask relative to what: peers, history or intrinsic value, then pick a method and state assumptions", "\"Yes.\"", "\"No.\"", "\"Look at the chart.\""], "Cheap is relative to an anchor."),
        T("<p>Rehearse mental math daily for five minutes. Two-digit products, percentages, fractions to decimals. It is the closest thing to a free skill upgrade.</p>"),
      ], "learn"),

      L("Follow-ups they throw at you", "fin.options", [
        T("<p>You gave the textbook answer; now they change one number. These follow-ups are commonly asked, and most are solved by <b>writing the formula and plugging in</b>. Check each by hand.</p>"),
        NUM("Pay $8 to roll two dice and receive the sum in dollars. Expected profit?", -1, "E[sum] = 7, so profit is 7 - 8 = -1 dollar.", null, 0, "$"),
        NUM("Roll a fair die until you get a six. Expected number of rolls?", 6, "Expected trials for success probability p is 1/p = 6.", null, 0, "rolls"),
        NUM("A 2-year bond has a face value of 100, an annual 5% coupon and a 4% yield. Price (two decimals)?", 101.89, "5/1.04 + 105/1.04^2 = 4.8077 + 97.0784 = 101.8861.", null, 0.02),
        NUM("A 3-year zero-coupon bond, face $1,000, 5% yield. Price (nearest dollar)?", 864, "1,000 / 1.05^3 = 1,000 / 1.157625 = 863.84.", null, 1, "$"),
        NUM("A bond has modified duration 7. Yields rise by 1 percentage point. Approximate price change in percent?", -7, "Price change is about -duration x change in yield = -7 x 1% = -7%.", null, 0, "%"),
        NUM("A strategy returns 12%, cash earns 2%, and volatility is 20%. Sharpe ratio?", 0.5, "(12 - 2) / 20 = 0.5.", null, 0.01),
        NUM("A daily Sharpe ratio is 0.1. Annualize with 252 trading days (two decimals).", 1.59, "0.1 x sqrt(252) = 0.1 x 15.87 = 1.587.", null, 0.01),
        NUM("Strike 100, premium paid 4, the stock finishes at 108. Profit on one long call share?", 4, "Payoff is 8, minus the 4 premium, leaves 4.", null, 0, "$"),
        NUM("Long a put: strike 50, premium 3, stock ends at 42. Profit per share?", 5, "Payoff is 50 - 42 = 8, minus 3 premium = 5.", null, 0, "$"),
        NUM("A straddle at strike 100 costs 5 for the call and 4 for the put. Upper breakeven price?", 109, "Total cost 9, so the stock must exceed 100 + 9 = 109.", null, 0, "$"),
        NUM("Put-call parity with zero rates: call 7, put 4, stock 100. What is the strike?", 97, "C - P = S - K gives K = 100 - 3 = 97.", null, 0, "$"),
        NUM("$1 grows 10% per year for 20 years. Value in dollars (two decimals)?", 6.73, "1.1^20 is about 6.7275.", null, 0.01, "$"),
        NUM("After a 50% loss, what percent gain is needed to get back to even?", 100, "Half of the money must double: +100%.", null, 0, "%"),
        NUM("Returns of +20% then -10%. The geometric average annual return, in percent (two decimals)?", 3.92, "1.2 x 0.9 = 1.08, and sqrt(1.08) - 1 is about 3.92%.", null, 0.02, "%"),
        NUM("A market maker quotes 99.90 / 100.10 and trades 1,000 shares on each side. Spread earned in dollars?", 200, "Each round trip earns 0.20, and 1,000 x 0.20 = $200.", null, 0, "$"),
        NUM("1% of transactions are fraud. A test catches 90% of fraud and falsely flags 9% of legitimate ones. If a transaction is flagged, probability it is fraud, in percent (one decimal)?", 9.2, "0.009 / (0.009 + 0.0891) = 0.0917, about 9.2%.", null, 0.1, "%"),
        Q("\"What is an at-the-money call's delta, roughly?\"", ["About 0.5", "About 0", "About 1", "About -0.5"], "At the money, the option has about even odds of finishing in the money."),
        M("Match each Greek to what it measures.", [["Delta", "Option price change per $1 move in the underlying"], ["Gamma", "Change in delta per $1 move in the underlying"], ["Theta", "Value lost per day as time passes"], ["Vega", "Price change per 1 point change in implied volatility"]], "Four sensitivities every options discussion uses."),
        RK("Rank by sensitivity to a rise in interest rates, most sensitive first.", ["30-year zero-coupon bond", "10-year 3% coupon bond", "2-year 3% coupon bond", "3-month Treasury bill"], "Longer maturity and lower coupon mean higher duration."),
        BN("Higher or lower duration?", ["Higher duration", "Lower duration"], [["Longer maturity", 0], ["Lower coupon", 0], ["Zero-coupon structure", 0], ["Shorter maturity", 1], ["Higher coupon", 1], ["Floating-rate coupon", 1]], "Duration is the weighted average time to cash flows."),
        O("Order a hygienic backtest process.", ["State the hypothesis before looking", "Split time into train, validation and a final holdout", "Include costs, slippage and capacity limits", "Test out-of-sample, once", "Report how many variants were tried"], "Each step blocks a typical way backtests lie."),
        BUG("Tap the line that miscomputes a drawdown-style return series.", ["wealth = 1.0", "for r in returns:", "    wealth = wealth * r", "print(wealth)"], 2, "Each period multiplies by (1 + r), not r."),
        F("Fill in the Kelly-style fraction for an even-odds bet.", "With win probability p and even odds, the fraction f* = ___ p - 1. With p = 0.6, f* = ___.", ["2", "0.2"], ["3", "0.6", "0.4"], "f* = 2p - 1. This is a textbook model, not a recommendation."),
        Q("\"Is implied volatility usually higher or lower than later realized volatility?\"", ["Often somewhat higher on average, reflecting a premium for bearing volatility risk, though it varies","Always lower, because option sellers consistently underestimate how much the underlying will actually move","Always equal, because the market prices options so that implied and realized volatility match in every period","Unrelated, because implied volatility reflects only supply and demand and has no link to later price movements"], "Evidence suggests a typical variance risk premium, with plenty of exceptions."),
        Q("\"A strategy has a Sharpe of 3 in backtest.\"", ["Be skeptical: look for overfitting, lookahead, unrealistic fills and capacity limits before believing it","It is certainly profitable, since a Sharpe ratio that high would be impossible to get from chance or from a flawed test","Scale it up immediately to the largest size you can, since a Sharpe of 3 means the risk is small next to the return","It beats every fund in the industry, so you can treat it as the best strategy available to anyone"], "Very high Sharpes are rare in the real world."),
        Q("\"What is convexity of a bond?\"", ["How duration changes with yield: price falls less than the linear estimate when yields rise, gains more when they fall","A bond's coupon schedule, meaning how the interest payments are arranged from the issue date to the maturity of the bond","Its credit rating, meaning how likely the issuer is to default relative to other bonds of a similar maturity","Its maturity date, meaning the length of time remaining until the principal is repaid to the holder of the bond"], "Convexity is the curvature correction to duration."),
        T("<p>If one of these felt slow, that is normal. Write each formula on paper and redo three of them without looking until the formulas are automatic.</p>"),
      ], "drill"),

      L("Mock loop", "fin.risk", [
        T("<p>Mock round: a mix of mental math, probability, bonds, options and market sense. Talk through each, then check.</p>"),
        NUM("Round 1: you pay $5 to play. You flip two fair coins and win $4 for each head. Expected profit?", -1, "Expected heads = 1, so expected winnings $4 against a $5 cost: -1.", null, 0, "$"),
        Q("Round 2: why might a bond with a lower coupon be more sensitive to rate changes than an otherwise equal bond with a higher coupon?", ["More of its value comes from the final principal payment far in the future, so its duration is longer","Lower coupons signal a riskier issuer, so investors apply a higher discount rate and the price becomes more volatile","The lower coupon is paid more often during the year, so the bond reprices more frequently when rates change","It has a smaller face value, so a given rate move changes a larger share of the money that is invested in it"], "Cash flows farther out are more rate sensitive."),
        NUM("Round 3: a 1-year zero-coupon bond has face 100 and yield 4%. Price (two decimals)?", 96.15, "100 / 1.04 = 96.1538.", null, 0.01),
        RK("Round 4: rank these from smallest to largest maximum possible loss (typical numbers).", ["Long call (premium paid)", "Short put (strike minus premium)", "Short uncovered call (no cap)"], "A long call loses at most its premium; a short put can lose up to the strike less the premium; an uncovered call has no ceiling."),
        M("Round 5: match each pitfall to its fix.", [["Survivorship bias", "Use point-in-time constituents including delisted names"], ["Lookahead bias", "Lag signals so they use only past data"], ["Data snooping", "Hold out a final test and count trials"], ["Ignoring costs", "Model spreads, fees and impact"]], "Each flaw has a standard remedy."),
        BUG("Round 6: tap the line that mis-annualizes this Sharpe ratio.", ["excess = daily_returns - rf_daily", "mean = excess.mean() * 252", "vol = excess.std()", "sharpe = mean / vol"], 2, "Mean was annualized by 252 but volatility was not. Volatility scales with the square root: vol = excess.std() * sqrt(252)."),
        Q("Round 7: a strategy earns steady small gains and rarely a huge loss. What should you ask?", ["How does it behave in tail events, and what are the drawdown and capacity limits?","How many winning days did it have last year, and how long was the longest streak of profitable days in a row?","What is the average daily profit, and how does it compare with that of similar strategies?","Does it trade often, and how large is the average position compared with typical daily volume?"], "Selling insurance-like payoffs looks smooth until it does not."),
        NUM("Round 8: a straddle costs 12 at strike 200. The stock finishes at 180. Profit?", 8, "Put payoff is 20 and total cost is 12, so profit is 8.", null, 0, "$"),
        O("Round 9: order how to handle a market-making inventory problem.", ["Notice inventory is getting one-sided", "Skew quotes to attract the opposite flow", "Hedge residual exposure in a related instrument", "Widen spreads if flow looks informed", "Review the cause after the session"], "Skew first, hedge second, widen if you suspect informed flow."),
        Q("Round 10: \"Would you take a bet with positive expected value but a chance of ruin?\"", ["Not blindly: size it so a loss cannot be fatal, since long-run growth depends on surviving","Always, since a positive expected value means the bet wins in the long run, so any size is justified by the average","Never take risky bets, since any chance of losing everything is unacceptable compared with a safe, certain payoff","Only if the payoff is large enough, since a big enough reward compensates for any probability of being wiped out"], "Ruin risk breaks the logic of repeated EV maximization."),
        Q("Round 11: closing question: \"What is the main thing you took from your last project or study?\"", ["A concrete example of forming a hypothesis, testing it honestly and acting on the result, including what failed","That you are deeply interested in markets and have followed them closely for years, reading about each major event","That you work hard and put in long hours, and that you will bring the same effort to any problem the team gives you","That you read many books on trading and finance, and can recall the key ideas of each one when they are relevant"], "Specific evidence of rigor beats generic enthusiasm."),
        T("<p>That is a full loop. Educational use only; none of this is investment advice. Good luck out there.</p>"),
      ], "pr"),
    ],
  });
})();
